    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search only covers seven days, so earlier misuse is not excluded. It is also not established that the burst was misuse rather than the owner acting.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst (new proxy, new fingerprint, payout changes) happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes, so the signals already existed on the platform when it ran. This assumes the check reads the platform's own request and account-change records.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link worth following, but it could equally be a common processor, a mule account, or one fraudster's destination attached to several victims.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** A diverted payout would usually prompt a complaint within 30 days, so silence is some evidence of legitimacy. It is weak because reports lag and nobody may have looked.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout changes, is a clear trigger for investigation regardless of how it turns out.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** The recorded rate is 0 of 100 for D against 2 of 100 for each of the others. The difference is too small to mean much statistically, but the rate as shown is lower.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined figure says nothing about the spread; one account could be well above 5 percent while others are below.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search ran across all merchant accounts, so its scope matches the claim. It only covers accounts held under that identity, not ones opened through nominees.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; we do not know whether the second saw the first note or whether both worked from the same model output.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment never completed, so it could not generate a chargeback; the absence of one says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared template